#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c976b6a-4523-51e1-a243-27aa8937db25" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Relationship Between the Rows of Pascal's Triangle in Terms of Binomial Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b3b1495-fd4e-590e-abf2-f1d01bed44e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245535} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d0e7ca-9cf6-56d2-bd14-35cdeb9caa81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245807} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a33636-8960-5f5f-8599-a5cf57881efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{6}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246060} {:answer/id #uuid "caa06872-b168-58f8-a4a8-ab8168991c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246113} {:answer/id #uuid "ce63f89f-656b-512a-96b3-aa29379755eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b3b1495-fd4e-590e-abf2-f1d01bed44e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245535} :answer-field/id #uuid "dafab0b3-0ea9-52f1-b23d-e401a912ba3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7e46def-b53d-544e-a445-f2ab7d8b4fbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-113611" :question/problem "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\frac{6}{4})=[]+(\\frac{5}{4})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $(\\frac{6}{4}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $6$ and position $4$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\frac{6}{4})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\frac{3}{2}) &  & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{4}{0}) &  & (\\frac{4}{1}) &  & (\\frac{4}{2}) &  & (\\frac{4}{3}) &  & (\\frac{4}{4}) \\\\  &  &  & (\\frac{5}{0}) &  & (\\frac{5}{1}) &  & (\\frac{5}{2}) &  & (\\frac{5}{3}) &  & (\\frac{5}{4}) &  & (\\frac{5}{5}) \\\\  &  & (\\frac{6}{0}) &  & (\\frac{6}{1}) &  & (\\frac{6}{2}) &  & (\\frac{6}{3}) &  & (\\frac{6}{4}) &  & (\\frac{6}{5}) &  & (\\frac{6}{6}) \\\\  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\frac{6}{4})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\frac{6}{4})=(\\frac{5}{3})+(\\frac{5}{4})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\frac{5}{3}).$"}]}
